---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import speaking from '../../assets/images/speaking.jpg';
---

<Layout title="About — wrap variants" description="Mock-up route." noindex>
	<div class="wrap">
		<header class="page-head">
			<h1>Wrap variants</h1>
			<p class="page-intro">
				D keeps the rule whole and moves it down; D2 lets the figure hang past
				a truncated rule; C floats it inside the prose. B is eliminated.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="vd">
			<p class="tag">D &mdash; figure in the header, rule unbroken, prose back to 672</p>
			<div class="vd-head">
				<div>
					<h2 class="mock-h1">About</h2>
					<p class="page-intro">Brief biography, with full C.V.&nbsp;available.</p>
				</div>
			<figure class="fig">
				<Image class="fig-img" src={speaking} alt="" widths={[224, 336, 448]} sizes="13rem" />
			</figure>
			</div>
			<div class="vd-bio">
				<p>
					I have served on the faculty of the English department at Western Oregon
					University since fall 1999. Alongside literature I teach classes in film studies, plus
					broader literary history and critical theory. Web design, digital
					archiving, and the digital humanities are closely related interests. I also
					directed our university Honors&nbsp;Program from 2008 until its closure
					in 2026.
				</p>
				<p>
					Western Oregon University is a public liberal arts college with an
					undergraduate enrollment of approximately 3,000 students. Founded in 1856
					by pioneers, it is the oldest public university in the state and one of the
					oldest on the West&nbsp;Coast.
				</p>
				<p>
					I specialize in twentieth-century English and Irish literature, especially
					fiction of the post-World War II period. My scholarly projects are
					oriented to both general and specialized audiences.
				</p>
				<p>
					Major projects include a book,{' '}
					<em>
						Father and Son: Kingsley Amis, Martin&nbsp;Amis, and the British Novel
						Since 1950
					</em>{' '}
					(University of Wisconsin, 2003 and 2005), as well as two edited
					collections:{' '}
					<em>Martin Amis: Postmodernism and Beyond</em> (Palgrave Macmillan, 2006)
					and <em>The Cambridge&nbsp;Companion to Martin&nbsp;Amis</em> (Cambridge
					University Press, 2026). All three are described on the{' '}
					<a href="/books/">Books</a> page.
				</p>
				<p>
					<em>The Cambridge&nbsp;Companion to Martin&nbsp;Amis</em> features the work
					of fourteen scholars from five different countries and is the definitive
					survey of Amis&rsquo;s fifty-year career. The book was spotlighted at a
					full-day{' '}
					<a href="https://martinamisweb.com/after-amis/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>book launch and podcast event</a
					>{' '}in London, with panels featuring seven of
					my fourteen contributors.
				</p>
				<p>
					Since 2006 I have also overseen and administered{' '}
					<a href="https://martinamisweb.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the Martin&nbsp;Amis Web</a>, the
					authoritative digital archive for that author. The British Library
					designated it a site of scholarly significance in 2012 and archives it
					every six months as part of their ongoing UK Web Archive Project.
				</p>
				<p>
					A novel, <em>Flight</em>, has been perhaps my most meaningful endeavor. The
					manuscript is complete at 80,000 words and currently under agent review.
				</p>
				<p>
					My final book project will be a scholarly monograph on spatial trauma and
					the reclamation of traumatic spaces in Ireland. Specific chapters will
					address Magdalene Laundries, Mother and Baby Homes, Ghost Estates, and
					Famine Walls/<wbr />Roads/<wbr />Graveyards.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

